changed. Nice touch: implement `/api/version` included in responses.\n\nDesign concept: \"Terminal-brutalist meets editorial\" — Let me pick something memorable: dark-first \"graphite + amber\" with mono accents, big type, thin borders, subtle grid, language colors as dots. Light theme: warm paper (#faf8f5). Accent: electric indigo #5b5bd6? Let's do \"Signal\" palette: ink #0b0c0f, paper #f7f5f2, accent #7c5cff (violet) + secondary accent lime #c8f169? Maybe more refined: accent teal #17b978. Hmm.\n\nI'll go with: palette — paper `#f5f3ef`, ink `#111214`, accent `#5a4bff` (indigo) for light; dark: bg `#0c0d10`, surface `#14161b`, accent `#a78bfa`. Typography: system stack + variable fonts served from... no external fonts (offline-friendly). Use font stack: ui-sans-serif with fallback, and monospace for metadata: \"JetBrains Mono\", ui-monospace fallback. Design idea: \"editorial grid with mono marginalia\" — cards with hairline borders, numbered index, language dot, tabular numbers, hover lift.\n\nFor SSR template with askama: need to render page. Include inline CSS via include_str? Serve static files via tower-http ServeDir — need \"fs\" feature. I included set-header/timeout but not fs. Let me add \"fs\" feature and serve /static. Also embed CSS into template to avoid FOUC — simpler: `<link rel=\"stylesheet\" href=\"/static/app.css\">`. Use ServeDir.\n\nAskama: templates dir \"templates\". index.html renders profile + repos SSR for SEO, then JS enhances.\n\nNow think about the sync/async details carefully to make it compile with no warnings, clippy clean.\n\nKey modules:\n\nconfig.rs:\n```rust\nuse std::time::Duration;\nuse anyhow::{bail, Context, Result};\n\n#[derive(Debug, Clone)]\npub struct Config {\n    pub username: String,\n    pub github_token: Option<String>,\n    pub refresh_interval: Duration,\n    pub port: u16,\n    pub admin_token: Option<String>,\n    pub pinned_repos: Vec<String>,\n    pub excluded_repos: Vec<String>,\n    pub show_forks: bool,\n    pub show_archived: bool,\n    pub cache_file: PathBuf,\n    pub max_repos: usize,\n    pub api_base: String,\n    pub cors_allow_origin: Option<String>,\n    pub readme_preview: bool? \n}\nimpl Config {\n  pub fn from_env() -> Result<Self>\n  fn env_parse<T: FromStr>(key, default) -> Result<T>\n}\n```\nEnv parsing: helper functions `opt_var`, `parse_var<T>` returning anyhow error.\n\ngithub.rs:\n```rust\npub struct GitHubClient { http: reqwest::Client, base: String, token: Option<String>, username: String }\n\npub enum Fetch<T> { Fresh { value: T, etag: Option<String> }, NotModified }\n\nimpl GitHubClient {\n  pub fn new(...) -> Result<Self>\n  async fn get_conditional<T: DeserializeOwned>(&self, url: &str, etag: Option<&str>) -> Result<Fetch<T>>\n}\n```\nError type: GitHubError with variants: NotModified handled separately, RateLimited { reset_at }, NotFound, Unauthorized, Http(StatusCode), Transport(reqwest::Error), etc. Use thiserror.\n\nRate limit: check `x-ratelimit-remaining: 0` → return RateLimited with reset. Also 403 with remaining 0 or 429.\n\nConditional fetch: pass `If-None-Match`; 304 → NotModified.\n\nPagination: `/users/{u}/repos?per_page=100&page={p}&sort=updated&type=owner`, stop when fewer than 100 or page > max pages (e.g., 10 → 1000 repos cap; max_repos config).\n\nConditional pagination with ETag per page — store etags keyed by full URL. Good.\n\nLanguages: `/repos/{owner}/{repo}/languages` returns map<String, u64>. Conditional too. Cache per repo keyed with `updated_at`? Simpler: key by URL and store etag; but languages rarely change; still if repo updated we should refetch... ETag handles it (server returns new etag/data). So just conditional per URL. But we shouldn't call for all repos every refresh if we have etags — 304s still consume rate limit? Conditional requests returning 304 do NOT count against rate limit for GitHub REST (304 responses don't count). Actually GitHub docs: \"Making a conditional request and receiving a 304 response does not count against your rate limit.\" Correct. Nice.\n\nBut we still make many requests. Concurrency limit 8, and only for repos we care about (after filtering? no — stats over all repos, but we can limit to top N by size/stars, e.g. max_repos).\n\nLanguage colors: a static map for common languages in models/languages.rs? For frontend we need colors; provide `language_color` function with a decent map, fallback hash-based hue. Implement in Rust for SSR and in JS for client-side rendering. To avoid duplication, provide color in API payload (each repo gets `language_color`, each language stat gets `color`). Frontend uses given color. \n\ncache.rs:\n```rust\npub struct Snapshot {\n    pub profile: Profile,\n    pub repos: Vec<Repo>,\n    pub languages: Vec<LanguageStat>,\n    pub fetched_at: DateTime<Utc>,\n    pub version: String, // hash of contents\n}\n\npub struct Cache {\n    inner: RwLock<Option<Arc<Snapshot>>>,\n    etags: RwLock<HashMap<String, String>>,\n    path: Option<PathBuf>,\n}\nimpl Cache {\n  pub async fn load_from_disk(&self, path) -> anyhow::Result<bool>\n  pub async fn snapshot(&self) -> Option<Arc<Snapshot>>\n  pub async fn store(&self, snap: Snapshot) \n  pub async fn save_to_disk(&self)\n}\n```\nPersist file: `{\"saved_at\":..., \"snapshot\": {...}, \"etags\": {...}}`.\n\nUse tokio::fs for disk IO. Write atomically: write to tmp then rename.\n\nrefresher: module `refresh.rs` (or inside github/cache). Function:\n```rust\npub async fn refresh(state: &AppState) -> Result<RefreshOutcome, GitHubError>\n```\nSteps:\n1. fetch profile (conditional).\n2. fetch repos pages (conditional).\n3. merge: if NotModified use cached.\n4. compute filtered repos (excluded, fork/archived rules for display — actually store all repos, filter at query time).\n5. fetch languages for repos (concurrency).\n6. aggregate stats.\n7. build Snapshot, store, save to disk.\n\nHandling partial NotModified: keep previous snapshot data. Need previous snapshot for profile/repos. Store etags in cache and bodies in snapshot. If profile 304 → use old profile. Requires previous snapshot; if none, that's weird (first run) — then force unconditional refetch? If we load etags from disk we also load snapshot from same file, so consistent. I'll handle: if NotModified but no previous value → do unconditional retry (drop etag). Implement helper in refresher.\n\nSimplify: `fetch_user` returns Option<Profile> where None=not modified and caller resolves from old.\n\nSingle-flight: use tokio::sync::Semaphore (1 permit) or Mutex to ensure only one refresh at a time, so /api/refresh doesn't stampede. Use `Arc<Mutex<()>>`? Use `tokio::sync::Mutex` guard around refresh. I'll add `refresh_lock: Mutex<()>` in state; refresh acquires try_lock? For API refresh, take lock (await). For background, try_lock, skip if busy.\n\nroutes.rs: handlers:\n- index: Html render; if no snapshot yet → render loading page with skeleton, JS polls.\n- api_profile: Json\n- api_repos: query filter\n- api_languages\n- api_meta/version\n- health\n- refresh (POST, header x-admin-token or Authorization: Bearer)\n- SSE optional: skip; poll instead. Mention in README.\n\nError handling: AppError implements IntoResponse returning JSON for /api/* and plain text for pages. Simpler: all JSON error bodies `{\"error\": {\"message\":...}}` except index falls back gracefully.\n\nGraceful shutdown: tokio::signal::ctrl_c + SIGTERM (unix signal). Handle both with tokio::select!.\n\nStructured logging: tracing-subscriber EnvFilter, and optional JSON via LOG_FORMAT=json.\n\nNow static frontend: templates/index.html + static/app.css + static/app.js.\n\nFrontend features:\n- theme toggle persisted in localStorage, respects prefers-color-scheme.\n- fetch /api/repos every REFRESH_POLL seconds (say 60) with `If-None-Match`/version compare; re-render if changed.\n- client-side filter (language select, topic chips, search input debounce), sort select.\n- skeleton while loading, empty/error states.\n- stats: language bar (horizontal segmented bar with legend) + SVG donut? Keep horizontal stacked bar with legend, plus percent.\n- accessible: aria-live for updates, focus-visible, skip link.\n\nData injection: embed initial JSON in `<script type=\"application/json\" id=\"bootstrap\">`? Askama can render `{{ bootstrap_json|safe }}` — need `|safe` filter to avoid escaping. In askama 0.14, filters: `{{ json|safe }}`. Yes askama has `safe` filter.\n\nCareful: embedding JSON in script tag needs escaping of `</script`. Use `serde_json::to_string` and replace `</` with `<\\/`. Do it in Rust.\n\nLet's write models:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub struct GitHubUser {\n    pub login: String,\n    pub id: u64,\n    pub name: Option<String>,\n    pub avatar_url: Option<String>,\n    pub html_url: Option<String>,\n    pub bio: Option<String>,\n    pub company: Option<String>,\n    pub blog: Option<String>,\n    pub location: Option<String>,\n    pub twitter_username: Option<String>,\n    pub public_repos: Option<u32>,\n    pub public_gists: Option<u32>,\n    pub followers: u32,\n    pub following: u32,\n    pub created_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub updated_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    #[serde(default)] pub hireable: Option<bool>,\n}\n```\nMake numbers Option? GitHub always includes; but for safety use `#[serde(default)]` on plain numbers. Followers u32 with default.\n\nRepo:\n```rust\npub struct GitHubRepo {\n  pub id: u64,\n  pub name: String,\n  pub full_name: String,\n  pub description: Option<String>,\n  pub html_url: String,\n  pub homepage: Option<String>,\n  pub language: Option<String>,\n  pub stargazers_count: u32,\n  pub forks_count: u32,\n  pub topics: Vec<String> (default),\n  pub fork: bool (default),\n  pub archived: bool (default),\n  pub pushed_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n  pub updated_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n  pub created_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n  pub size: u64 default,\n  pub open_issues_count default,\n  pub default_branch: Option<String>,\n  pub license: Option<License>,\n  pub watchers_count? skip\n}\n```\nThen transformed `Repo` (domain) with additional `language_color`, `languages: Vec<LanguageSlice>`? Keep languages in a separate map for stats. Domain Repo:\n\n```rust\npub struct Repo {\n    pub id: u64, pub name: String, pub full_name: String,\n    pub description: Option<String>, pub html_url: String, pub homepage: Option<String>,\n    pub language: Option<String>, pub language_color: Option<String>,\n    pub stars: u32, pub forks: u32, pub topics: Vec<String>,\n    pub is_fork: bool, pub is_archived: bool,\n    pub updated_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub pushed_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub created_at: Option<DateTime<Utc>>,\n    pub open_issues: u32,\n    pub license: Option<String>,\n    pub pinned: bool,\n    pub bytes_languages: usize? // maybe not; keep total bytes\n    pub languages: Vec<LanguageShare>? // per-repo breakdown — used in card tooltip? skip\n}\n```\nAlso provide `updated_human` computed in template via helper methods: `updated_human()` returns \"2 дня назад\" — Russian? The site language: user writes Russian. I'll make the UI Russian (with English-ish accents) since the prompt is Russian. Hmm, portfolio could be bilingual. I'll do Russian UI text with locale-friendly date formatting done in JS for relative time, and SSR fallback \"2024-05-01\". Let me do relative time formatting in Rust (Russian) and in JS.\n\nRust relative time in Russian requires pluralization: \"3 дня назад\", \"5 месяцев назад\". Implement helper with plural rules. Do it in `util.rs` / `models.rs` impl.\n\nLanguage colors: build a map of ~90 common languages. I'll write a compact match returning &'static str for popular ones (Rust, TypeScript, JavaScript, Python, Go, Java, C, C++, C#, Ruby, PHP, Swift, Kotlin, Dart, Shell, HTML, CSS, SCSS, Vue, Svelte, Elixir, Erlang, Haskell, Lua, R, Scala, Clojure, Zig, Nim, Julia, Objective-C, Perl, PowerShell, Dockerfile, Makefile, Nix, SQL, TeX, Assembly, Solidity, OCaml, F#, Vim Script, GDScript, Astro, MDX, HCL, TOML, Jinja, Handlebars, Jupyter Notebook, Emacs Lisp, Common Lisp, Crystal, Vala, Ada, Fortran, COBOL, Prolog, Scheme, Racket, Elm, PureScript, ReasonML, Groovy, Visual Basic .NET, MATLAB, Wolfram, V, Odin, Gleam, WebAssembly, Smarty, Blade, EJS, CMake, Batchfile, AutoHotkey, AppleScript, CoffeeScript, SCSS, Less, Stylus, Markdown) fallback: deterministic HSL from name hash → return format!(\"hsl({hue} 62% 52%)\") — needs String, so return String from function.\n\nSignature: `pub fn language_color(lang: &str) -> String`.\n\nNow stats: aggregate `HashMap<String, u64>` bytes → LanguageStat { name, bytes, percent, color, repos: count }.\n\nPersistence of `bytes` fine.\n\nFiltering logic in `filters.rs` (module name \"filters\"). Functions:\n\n```rust\n#[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq, Serialize, Deserialize)]\npub enum SortKey { Stars, Updated, Name, Forks }\nimpl SortKey { from_str }\n\npub struct FilterOptions { pub query: Option<String>, pub language: Option<String>, pub topics: Vec<String>, pub include_forks: bool, pub include_archived: bool, pub sort: SortKey }\n\npub fn apply(repos: &[Repo], opts: &FilterOptions) -> Vec<Repo>\n```\nPinned first regardless of sort; then sort rest. Also `normalize_str`.\n\nQuery params parsing from axum `Query<RepoQueryParams>` with serde Deserialize, `language`, `q`, `topic` (repeatable), `sort`, `forks`, `archived` (bool/`1`). Use Option<String> to keep tolerant parsing? serde with bool from \"true\"/\"1\"? Use Option<String> and parse manually to be tolerant. I'll implement `RepoQueryParams { q, language, topic: Option<String>, sort, include_forks: Option<String>, include_archived: Option<String>, limit: Option<usize> }` and a conversion fn -> Result<FilterOptions, AppError>.\n\nAxum 0.8: `Query<T>` requires serde::Deserialize. Fine.\n\nNote axum 0.8 path syntax `/{param}` instead of `/:param`. We have no path params except maybe none. Good — routes: \"/\", \"/api/profile\", \"/api/repos\", \"/api/languages\", \"/api/meta\", \"/api/refresh\", \"/health\", \"/static\" nested service.\n\nNow tests: fixtures JSON in `tests/fixtures/user.json`, `repos.json`, `languages.json`; integration tests hitting parsing/filter/sort. Also router tests with tower::ServiceExt::oneshot? That requires state without network. Could test /health and /api/repos with pre-populated cache. Let's include a couple integration tests in tests/api.rs using axum app with a cache loaded from JSON snapshot file — doable: build Cache, store snapshot, build router, oneshot. Good demonstration. But careful about API surface for tests: expose `github_portfolio::{config, cache, routes,...}` — needs lib.rs + main.rs. Yes: create `src/lib.rs` exposing modules, and `src/main.rs` thin binary using the lib. That's cleaner for tests.\n\napp.rs: `pub fn build_router(state: AppState) -> Router`.\n\nNote: template rendering requires templates dir at compile time; askama uses `templates/` relative to CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR by default. Fine.\n\nFor static files: `ServeDir::new(static_dir)` with `set_response_header(CACHE_CONTROL)`. tower-http \"fs\" feature needed.\n\nLet me now also consider: `#[derive(Template)]` struct in routes or separate `templates.rs` module. I'll put page structs in `src/page.rs` or inside routes.rs. I'll create `src/templates.rs` with `IndexTemplate`.\n\nAskama 0.14: `askama::Template` derive; render() -> Result<String, askama::Error>. Map error to AppError.\n\nOk. Let's also handle the \"site builds automatically\" — the SSR page uses current snapshot; JS polls.\n\nSSE: skip, but I could add it easily... Requirement says \"либо\" (or). Polling chosen.\n\nAlso `/api/meta` returns refresh interval, username, last fetch, next scheduled, config flags.\n\nRate limit handling: on RateLimited, log warn, return last cache. Also expose headers `x-ratelimit-remaining` maybe. Add to meta: `rate_limit_remaining`.\n\nLet me keep a `RateLimit` struct in state updated after each request: `Arc<RwLock<Option<RateLimitInfo>>>`? Simpler: return it from refresher into state. I'll include in Snapshot? No — store in state `Mutex<Option<RateLimit>>`. Might be extra complexity; but nice touch. I'll include `rate_limit` in AppState as `RwLock<Option<RateLimit>>` set during refresh, exposed in /api/meta. Keep it.\n\nNow health endpoint: returns JSON {status:\"ok\", cache_age_secs, repos, last_success}. Should return 200 even if cache empty? If empty → status \"degraded\" with 200 (healthcheck must pass to keep container alive) — I'll return 200 with status \"starting\"/\"degraded\". Docker HEALTHCHECK uses /health with curl/wget. In debian-slim, no curl. Options: use distroless? Then healthcheck impossible without binary. Use debian-slim + install ca-certificates + curl? That adds size. Alternative: add a tiny healthcheck binary in the image using the app itself: `github-portfolio --healthcheck` mode that does a TCP/HTTP GET to localhost:PORT and exits 0/1. That's elegant and avoids curl. I'll implement: in main, if args contain `--healthcheck`, perform GET http://127.0.0.1:PORT/health with timeout 3s, exit code accordingly. Need ureq? Use reqwest blocking? reqwest without blocking feature... reqwest blocking requires \"blocking\" feature. Instead spawn tokio runtime and async request. Fine: keep it async with `#[tokio::main]` and early branch.\n\nBut healthcheck needs PORT env. Compose passes env. Good. Also allow `HEALTHCHECK_URL`. I'll implement with reqwest using tokio current_thread.\n\nFinal image: debian:bookworm-slim + ca-certificates, non-root user, tini? Not needed if we handle signals in Rust (we do). Add `--init` in compose? Not necessary. Keep simple: use `debian:bookworm-slim` with tini installed? I'll skip tini since Rust handles SIGTERM.\n\nDockerfile multi-stage with cargo-chef? Without cargo-chef: copy Cargo.toml/Cargo.lock, create dummy main, build deps (cargo build --release), then copy src and